<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Chinese translation, in **Simplified characters**, mainland vocabulary.

    Translated from `values/strings.xml`, which is the source (SPEC §9). Its
    comments are carried over here, so that whoever touches a string next
    reads what its placeholders stand for and on which screen it appears.

    The folder is `values-zh`, with no region: Android serves it to every
    Chinese reader whose more precise folder is missing, and its own default
    for a bare `zh` is Simplified. Writing Traditional here would reach a
    Hong Kong or Taiwan reader in their script and a Beijing one in the
    wrong one, where Simplified reaches the largest readership correctly and
    leaves `values-zh-rTW` and `values-zh-rHK` free for whoever writes them.
    The vocabulary follows the same region: 自行车 rather than 單車,
    存储空间 rather than 儲存空間, 应用 rather than 應用程式 — and the
    Simplified Android lexicon throughout.

    The typography is Chinese:
      · no spaces between words, and full-width punctuation — ，。：、（）
        「」“” — which already carries the space a Latin mark would need
        beside it, so nothing is padded around it;
      · figures, unit symbols and Latin names stay half-width, and a
        **half-width space separates Chinese characters from a Latin run or
        a figure**: 「仅通过 WLAN 下载」, 「GBFS 格式」, 「ODbL 许可」,
        「版本 %1$s」, 「第 %1$d 步，共 %2$d 步」. That is what Android's own
        Simplified strings do — 「%1$d 个应用正在消耗电量」, 「Enter 键」 —
        and a placeholder follows the same rule by what it **holds**: a city,
        a station, an address, a size or a count takes the space, a fragment
        this file wrote itself does not — 「设置%1$s」, where %1$s is 家;
      · the space between a figure and the unit or classifier naming it is a
        **non-breaking one** (U+00A0), here as in every language:
        `UnitTypographyTest` holds the whole file to it, so that a summary
        never ends a line on 「5」 and opens the next on 「m 爬升」;
      · a question keeps its mark, and the mark is the full-width ？;
      · a quoted name is set in the full-width “ ”, tight, as Android writes
        「已将“%1$s”复制到剪贴板。」;
      · the separator the English writes `·` is kept as `·` (U+00B7), which
        is the interpunct a Chinese reader already knows, with the ordinary
        spaces the source gives it;
      · **Wi-Fi is written WLAN.** Simplified Android says WLAN wherever the
        English says Wi-Fi — `settings:wifi` is 「WLAN」 — and the switch that
        names it here has to be the word the reader will look for in their
        own settings;
      · no apostrophe is written anywhere, so nothing here is escaped;
      · Chinese has **one plural category, `other`**, and CLDR gives it no
        other. A noun does not agree in number, so the single item reads
        correctly for nought, one and a thousand alike — but only if the
        **classifier** is right, and that is where the work went: 辆 for
        bikes, 个 for docking places and for stations, 秒 · 分钟 · 小时 · 天 ·
        个月 for the age of the data;
      · the register is neutral and plain, addressing the reader as 您 where
        a pronoun is unavoidable and dropping it where Chinese would. SPEC §9
        asks the French for `tu`; Chinese has no such choice to make, and
        Android's own Simplified strings write 您.

    The words settled on, and why, are in docs/glossary/zh.md. See
    CONTRIBUTING.md to submit a correction.
-->
